      "ballot_id": "B024",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Daniel S. Fisher",
        "James P. Sethna",
        "Karin A. Dahmen"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "A powerful account of crackling noise that identifies universal avalanche dynamics across disordered systems. The combination of general theory and broad explanatory reach is exceptionally strong.",
        "attribution": "The credited theorists represent complementary contributions to depinning, scaling, and avalanche theory. A prize would need to recognize their distinct roles within a wider experimental and theoretical community.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been developed and tested across many systems over several decades, with ample time for its significance to settle.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery is a body of related work rather than one sharply bounded result, making the division of credit difficult."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B026",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Katepalli R. Sreenivasan",
        "Uriel Frisch"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Intermittency and multifractal structure exposed how strongly developed turbulence departs from simple scaling. This is a deep statistical description of a central, persistent problem in physics.",
        "attribution": "Frisch helped establish the theoretical and multifractal framework, while Sreenivasan made major contributions to measurement and characterization. Their joint credit captures theory and experiment, though the field has many important contributors.",
        "maturity": "The ideas have been tested against extensive measurements and remain central to turbulence research; the work is mature.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The broad scope of turbulence intermittency makes it hard to draw a precise boundary around the prize contribution and its recipients."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael Berry",
        "Yakir Aharonov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase revealed that quantum phases have observable geometric and topological content. These concepts reshaped understanding across quantum mechanics and many areas of physics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov is central to the effect demonstrating the physical role of potentials, while Berry identified a broadly applicable geometric phase. The two contributions are related but historically distinct.",
        "maturity": "Both ideas have been confirmed in many experimental settings and have proved durable well beyond their original contexts.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Combining two major discoveries under one award would require a careful account of their separate intellectual histories."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Stephen J. Parke",
        "Tomasz R. Taylor",
        "Zvi Bern"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "On-shell methods revealed unexpected simplicity in gauge-theory amplitudes and became important tools for precision collider predictions. The conceptual insight and practical reach are both substantial.",
        "attribution": "Parke and Taylor supplied a striking foundational result, while Bern helped develop the modern amplitude methods and their applications. The credit is plausible for the stated contribution but spans distinct stages of the field.",
        "maturity": "The methods have been intensively developed and applied over decades, including to demanding precision calculations.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "It is difficult to isolate the named contributions from the large international effort that made the methods broadly useful."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B019",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Kenneth J. Schafer",
        "Maciej Lewenstein",
        "Paul B. Corkum"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The three-step recollision model supplied a simple mechanism for high-harmonic generation and helped open attosecond science. It is an unusually clear example of theory guiding a new experimental regime.",
        "attribution": "Corkum's model and the complementary theoretical work of Schafer and Lewenstein make a credible trio for the nominated contribution. The broader experimental development involved many groups.",
        "maturity": "The model has been validated, extended, and used across decades of strong-field research.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The proposed discovery connects a theoretical mechanism to a larger experimental field whose contributions are not captured by three names."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B021",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Andrea J. Liu",
        "Sidney R. Nagel"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Jamming identified a transition from flowing to rigid behavior in disordered matter and connected a wide range of systems through shared physical ideas. Its broad influence is notable.",
        "attribution": "Liu and Nagel were central in establishing the jamming framework and its phase-diagram perspective. Credit for the surrounding work is broad, including key contributions from other researchers.",
        "maturity": "The concept has been studied for decades across experiments, simulations, and theory, though its relation to other forms of glassy arrest remains actively discussed.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The precise universality and boundaries of the jamming transition remain unsettled across different materials and protocols."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Rashid Sunyaev"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Sunyaev-Zel'dovich effects provide a fundamental link between hot plasma and the cosmic microwave background, enabling powerful studies of galaxy clusters and cosmology.",
        "attribution": "Sunyaev is the sole credited name, but the discovery is conventionally associated with a joint theoretical contribution. The supplied credit therefore appears incomplete.",
        "maturity": "The effects have been observed and exploited in many surveys, making the physical prediction exceptionally well established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The absence of another contributor to the original theoretical work makes attribution on this ballot uncertain."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B020",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alessandra Buonanno",
        "Frans Pretorius",
        "Thibault Damour"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Effective-one-body theory and numerical relativity made binary black-hole waveforms calculable and enabled gravitational-wave observations to test strong-field gravity. This is a major theoretical and computational achievement.",
        "attribution": "Buonanno and Damour developed the effective-one-body framework, while Pretorius achieved a landmark numerical breakthrough. The credited names map to distinct but complementary advances.",
        "maturity": "The methods have been validated against gravitational-wave observations and refined through many subsequent detections.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The nominated discovery combines two substantial lines of work and may underrepresent other groups essential to waveform modeling."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
